On Mon, 6 Dec 1999, Paul B. Brown wrote:

> >    (atleast ours) the difference isn=B4t that significant (since we can=
't
> >    get a hold of Intels internal compiler among other things).
>=20
> Hummm . . . So again, if code is developed with the Alpha in mind, the
> application will be faster than the same application coded specifically
> for the Intel.  OK . . . .

Not even, I have a piece of junk code which I use for testing ideas out
rather than performance, originally developed on Intel 486.

Smith and Waterman search of Swissprot 37 database using a 277 residue
protein sequence.

Intel Celeron 400 - 822 seconds =3D=3D 9 million cell updates/sec
Alpha 164LX   533 - 316 seconds =3D=3D 25 million cell updates/sec
Alpha 264EV67 667 - 164 seconds =3D=3D 46 million cell updates/sec

Looks like a pretty good win for the Alpha, even if you consider that the
Intel cost < 500 quid and the Alpha 164 was 1100 it's still better value.
The 264 is less easy to justify as those systems are priced much higher, I
was quoted 8000 for an XP1000 with 667 EV67 processor.  A cluster of 164
boxes would be a better bet for our work.  Even assuming you could get an
800Mhz x86 processor now you would still be looking at a performance
around 30% less than the 533 164 and rather shocking 60% down on a 667 264
box despite the apparent higher clock speed.

Alphas are good, very very good.

Shane Sturrock


