>=20
>=20
>=20
> Even the "Smart" Array 3200 is little more than a Compaq OEM=20
> of the DAC960
> PG with 2.x class firmware. =20
> It is for this reason that many consultants like myself may=20
> be tempted to
> flash the EEPROM up to a higher Mylex "retail" version when there is =
a
> second EEPROM seated in the second EEPROM chip seat.  Of=20
> course, this will
> end your warranty when done, though in certain very specific=20
> circumstances
> (or when you are at the end of your warranty), this can get=20
> you support for
> the 18.2, 24 and even 36 GB drive capacities that you may require.  =
Of
> course, remember the old water cooler anaology when building=20
> RAID arrays:
> the more spigots you have on your water cooler the faster you=20
> can drain (or
> fill) the water cooler.  Having a REALLY LARGE water cooler=20
> with only a few
> spigots gets you a high capacity per cm but not much in the way of
> read/write performance.  Somewhere in the middle is the right
> price/performance/capacity which can vary wildly with system=20
> or applicaiton
> requirements.  It is still preferable to build an array with=20
> 9GB building
> blocks (particularly the newer IBM Enterprise or Seagate=20
> Cheetah 2 drives).=20
>=20
> As a simple example, I have found that for large sequential=20
> read/write and
> random uncached read/write, the fastest RAID level by far is=20
> RAID "6" (in
> Mylex speak), also called RAID level 0/1 by the RAB (RAID=20
> Advisory Board).
>  RAID level 0/1, sometimes called RAID "10" is the RAID level=20
> in which you
> have two identical RAID 0 stripped arrays (consisting of two=20
> or more drives
> per RAID level 0 array), which is by definition a "mirrored=20
> stripe set"
> which is very different from a stripped mirror (you can=20
> research this at
> the RAB, if you wish).  At this RAID level, you do not use parity
> information which means there is no real performance hit when=20
> a drive is
> lost since parity isn't being used to generate missing data=20
> and there is an
> ENTIRE copy of the data stripped across the other array.  The=20
> effect of
> RAID 10 is high performance through multiplexing two RAID 0=20
> arrays (RAID 0
> has a high read/write performance due to stripped data across =
multiple
> disks with no parity generation overhead).  A properly=20
> configured RAID 10
> can be as much as 50% faster than a RAID 0 array of the same=20
> size, though
> you need twice as many disks to achieve the same capacity.
>=20
> Large stripe block sizes (say 128 KB) produce the best sequential
> read/write performance by "chunking" more data per write (and=20
> therefore
> fewer transactions are required) while smaller stripe blocks=20
> (say 8KB) are
> ideal for random access to such things as databases.  I turn=20
> write-back
> cache ON on the controller in all installations since the UPS that is
> installed is always more than enough for a 15 minute run,=20
> more than long
> enough to shutdown the system and commit to disk.  I don't=20
> bother stuffing
> more than about 32MB of memory (which is pretty cheap these=20
> days) since the
> performance difference between 8 MB of cache and 32 MB in=20
> most cases is
> less than 6%.  =20
>=20
> Where sequential read/write of large files is the design for=20
> the RAID, use
> read-ahead caching.  Most new RAID controllers have Adaptive cache
> configurations in which the cache can reconfigure itself into several
> smaller caches in order to take better advantage of the=20
> available cache for
> smaller more random traffic.  I am also pretty hellbent on=20
> getting even
> more performance by ENABLING the write cache on the drives=20
> themselves.  By
> default, most hard drives ship with their mode pages for write cache
> DISABLED to make them "safer."  If you are using a newer drive with a
> large, smart cache, take advantage of it in the the critical=20
> area of write
> performance and turn them on.  Note you will see NO=20
> performance increase in
> situations where the ratio of read to write time is greater=20
> than 75% (which
> is most of the time) but you will see a slight (5%)=20
> difference if your RAID
> spends most of its time being hit by a steady stream of write=20
> requests (say
> in log generation for a database with a long sequential stream of =
data
> writes). =20
>=20
> StorageWorks kits with a duplexer (adds channels to the stock=20
> backplane)
> can produce the ideal result though you may be able to buy a=20
> less expensive
> unit from the industrial computing market with similar or even =
greater
> capacity. =20
>=20
>=20
> Good luck,
>=20
> --Kirk
